# Complex UI/UX Patterns — IHS ERP

This skill defines the canonical UI/UX design patterns for building complex interactive execution boards, multi-step wizards, and data-dense matrices within IHS ERP (based on the gold standard established in `OmniPackingBoard`).

## 1. Multi-Step Execution Wizards (Cards)

When designing a complex workflow (e.g., Target Configuration -> Component Ratio -> Stock Matrix), break it down into sequential visual blocks using styled `<Card>` components.

### Pattern Rules
- **Progressive Disclosure**: Show subsequent steps only when prerequisites are met (e.g., Step 2 appears only after Step 1 is configured).
- **Active State Highlights**: Use subtle background colors (`bg-primary/5` or `bg-blue-500/5`) and colored borders (`border-primary/20`) to highlight the active or most critical step.
- **Clear Headers**: Always include a `CardTitle` with an Icon and a `CardDescription` explaining the step's purpose.

### Implementation Example
```tsx
<Card className="border-blue-500/20 shadow-lg">
    <CardHeader className="bg-blue-500/5">
        <CardTitle className="text-lg text-blue-500 flex items-center gap-2">
            <Layers className="w-5 h-5"/> Step 3: Deep Stock Analysis
        </CardTitle>
        <CardDescription>Review the recursive availability of your components across Pack and Loose states.</CardDescription>
    </CardHeader>
    <CardContent>
        {/* Complex Content */}
    </CardContent>
</Card>
```

## 2. Deep Analysis Matrices (Dense Tables)

For complex data grids showing stock, analysis, or multi-dimensional records, standard zebra-striping is not enough. You must use high-contrast color coding to guide the user's eyes.

### Pattern Rules
- **Header Structure**: Use `bg-muted text-muted-foreground uppercase text-xs font-black tracking-wider` for table headers.
- **Semantic Colors for Data**:
  - Positive/Ready data (e.g., Packed Stock): `text-emerald-600 font-bold`
  - Warning/Actionable data (e.g., Loose Stock needing conversion): `text-amber-600 font-bold`
  - Primary metric (e.g., Required Qty): `text-lg font-black`
- **Contextual Badges**: Use styled badges for status instead of plain text.
  - Ready: `<Badge variant="outline" className="bg-emerald-500/10 text-emerald-500 border-emerald-500/20 text-xs py-1"><CheckCircle2 className="w-4 h-4 mr-1"/> Ready</Badge>`
  - Action Required: `<Badge variant="outline" className="bg-amber-500/10 text-amber-500 border-amber-500/20 text-xs py-1"><Factory className="w-4 h-4 mr-1"/> Convert</Badge>`
  - Error/Shortage: `<Badge variant="destructive" className="text-xs py-1"><AlertTriangle className="w-4 h-4 mr-1"/> Shortage</Badge>`

## 3. Interactive Inline Trace Locations & Forms

When users need to select lots, batches, or overrides *inside* a table row, use inline cards/panels rather than separate modals.

### Pattern Rules
- **Sub-cards**: Wrap the interactive area in a nested styled div: `flex flex-col p-2 border rounded-md bg-background/50 mb-2 shadow-sm`.
- **Realtime Calculations Tooltips**: Always show the user *why* a calculation is happening inline (e.g., `≈ 80 pcs × 1 ÷ 50 = 1.60 ROLL`). Use muted text for this.
- **Formula Overrides**: Hide advanced overrides (like formula modifiers) behind a toggle/checkbox to keep the UI clean, but make them accessible inline.

### Implementation Example
```tsx
<div className="flex flex-col p-2 border rounded-md bg-background/50 mb-2 shadow-sm">
    <div className="flex items-center justify-between gap-3">
        <div className="flex flex-col">
            <span className="font-mono text-xs font-bold">{loc.lotNo}</span>
            <span className="text-[10px] text-muted-foreground">Available: {loc.qty}</span>
        </div>
        <div className="flex items-center gap-2">
            <span className="text-xs text-muted-foreground whitespace-nowrap">Enter Pcs:</span>
            <Input 
                type="number" 
                className="w-20 h-8 text-center font-bold" 
                value={selections[key] || ''} 
            />
        </div>
    </div>
    {/* Realtime Calc Text */}
    <div className="mt-1 flex justify-between items-center bg-emerald-500/10 p-1.5 rounded text-[10px] font-mono">
        <span className="text-emerald-700">Need: 80</span>
        <span className="text-emerald-700 font-bold">Yield: {calcString}</span>
    </div>
</div>
```

## 4. Master Action Buttons in Headers

If a table requires repetitive data entry (e.g., auto-filling locations, selecting all), place a "Master Action Button" inside the `<th>` tag.

### Pattern Rules
- Use a small, distinctly colored button (e.g., `bg-amber-200 text-amber-800 hover:bg-amber-300`).
- Ensure it sits flush with the column label using `flex justify-between items-center`.

### Implementation Example
```tsx
<th className="p-3 flex justify-between items-center">
    <span>Loose Locations (Trace)</span>
    <Button 
        type="button" 
        variant="secondary" 
        size="sm" 
        className="h-6 text-[10px] bg-amber-200 text-amber-800 hover:bg-amber-300 ml-2"
        onClick={handleMasterAutoFill}
    >
        MASTER AUTO-FILL
    </Button>
</th>
```

## 5. Complex Error Dialogs (No Toast Overload)

For complex validation failures (e.g., missing 5 pieces of stock across 3 different lots), **DO NOT use `toast.error`**. Toasts disappear too quickly and don't allow users to read long error logs. 

### Pattern Rules
- Use `AlertDialog` for complex execution errors.
- Format the error message with line breaks `\n\n` to separate context, values, and required actions.
- Preserve the user's state so they can fix the issue without losing their form data.

## 6. Dynamic Design Principles
- **Avoid Generic Colors**: Instead of flat `#fff`, use semantic transparent layers like `bg-primary/5` or `bg-muted/50`.
- **Micro-Animations**: Add `transition-all` on hoverable inline cards or inputs to make the interface feel responsive.
- **Glassmorphism Hints**: Use semi-transparent backgrounds with borders for deep nested elements to give a sense of depth and hierarchy.
